<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theophanes the Confessor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 760–<a href="/years/818">818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Theophanes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theophanes the Confessor was a Byzantine monk and chronicler whose Chronographia covers the history of the empire from 284 to 813. It is a key source for the 7th and 8th centuries, including the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. Exiled for opposing iconoclasm, he died on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> and was venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor.jpg" alt="Theophanes the Confessor" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tzortzis Phouka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athonite_Icon_of_Saint_Theophanes_the_Confessor_of_Megalos_Agros_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Byzantine chroniclers, chiefly <b>Theophanes</b>, say seven tribes there formed a union, though their names are not recorded.</blockquote><a href="/state/seven-slavic-tribes">Seven Slavic Tribes</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Bulgars arrived under Asparuh in 680–681, <b>Theophanes</b> records that the Severi were moved to guard the passes towards Byzantium, while the Seven Slavic Tribes held other borders.</blockquote><a href="/state/severians-balkan">Severians (Balkan)</a></li></ul></section>
